{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786281536,"nonce":"ZjL76ddm1ix6SW8OuCRl+0/RCEY=","title":"**What is wrong.** Roughly sixteen tables still declare `DEFAULT now()` on","message":"`created_at` / `updated_at` columns that **every production writer already names\nexplicitly** — `users`, `api_tokens`, `sessions`, `clusters`, `alert_sources`,\n`notification_policies`, `channel_threads`, `notifications`,\n`notification_deliveries`, and others.\n\nMigrations `00032` and `00033` dropped exactly these defaults from `channels`, `orgs`\nand `channel_credentials`, and both argued the same thing: a default that no live path\nexercises is not inert, it is **a trap with a delayed fuse.** It lets a future writer\nomit the column, succeed, and plant a row whose timestamp came from the database's clock\nwhile every other timestamp on that row came from the application's — which then fails\nmuch later, somewhere else, as a 500 blaming a CHECK constraint far from the mistake.\nWithout the default, the same omission fails at the offending statement as a not-null\nviolation, in the first test that runs it.\n\nThis is a consistency gap, not a live defect: no current writer relies on any of these\ndefaults.\n\n**Why it matters.** The argument for dropping them was accepted twice, in two migrations\nthat are now in history. Leaving sixteen tables on the other side of that argument means\nthe codebase disagrees with itself about who owns time, and the next person cannot tell\nwhich convention is intended. CONTEXT.md is explicit that the application owns time\n(`platform/clock` exists so time is injectable and testable); the surviving defaults are\nthe only thing still saying otherwise.\n\nNote this is **separate from** the monotonicity hazard, which is a live 500 rather than a\nlatent trap.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/`, every table with `DEFAULT now()` on a timestamp column that\na repository already supplies. `db/migrations/00033_orgs_and_credentials_app_clock.sql`\nis the reference for both the change and the Down.\n\n**Done when.** Either the remaining defaults are dropped in one expand/contract\nmigration, with any test seed that relied on them fixed to stamp a real clock; or a note\nin `docs/design/SPEC.md` §D records that these tables keep their defaults deliberately\nand says why the argument in `00032`/`00033` does not apply to them.","files":null}]}